# Compile semantic notebooks with measured flow and fixed regions

## Scope and existing evidence

Paste text or Markdown, preserve headings, paragraphs and lists as notebook
blocks, and paginate them onto the selected physical sheets. Expose overflow
and fixed-region refusals instead of silently clipping content.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P2-001 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-003 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-004 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-005 — archived semantic design

## Native CLI plain-text and Markdown flow (partial) — 2026-11-03

The Rust `atrament-profiles notebook` CLI now accepts an explicitly supplied
`--flow-text` or `--flow-markdown` source, or `--flow-text-file` and
`--flow-markdown-file` for caller-selected bounded UTF-8 files (maximum
4,096 bytes). It uses the selected saved paper profile's validated physical
printable rectangle, exact Unicode grapheme boundaries and the same nominal
per-glyph horizontal advances as the native authored-stroke compositor.

When a handwriting statistics profile is selected, the mean width/spacing
and height/baseline affect flow; without one, advances are 4 mm and the
minimum row pitch is 9 mm. Page breaks are automatic, up to 32 pages and
128 laid-out lines; the preexisting 512 total pen-segment export cap remains.
An explicit `--pages` smaller than the measured PDF count refuses.

The limited Markdown subset recognizes `#`, `##`, and `###` single-line
headings and `-`, `*` or `+` list items. Headings default to an explicit ink
underline, while `--heading-style marker` places an opaque colored highlight
**behind** authored heading ink; `none` suppresses the ornament without
changing text.

The physical decorations now distinguish heading hierarchy:
`#` uses a 0.55-mm underline or four 1.4-mm marker strokes, `##` a 0.42-mm
underline or three 1.15-mm strokes, and `###` a 0.30-mm underline or two
0.95-mm strokes. One new Rust integration regression checks the tiered
coordinates and ink weights, actual PDF/PNG output, color and deterministic
replay; first-level styling remains byte-compatible. List items keep their
physical marks and indentation. This is still CLI-only decoration, not
semantic styles or persistent title identities.

The unordered-list slice also accepts `+ ` alongside `- ` and `* `, with
identical synthetic pen dash ink and indentation for all three Markdown
markers; `+` remains literal in plain-text mode. A native Rust regression
checks exact PDF/PNG byte parity across all three spellings, preserved
paragraph boundaries, and refusal of empty `+ ` without replacing output.

Plain-text lines wrap independently at word or grapheme boundaries;
Markdown ordinary lines now combine as soft-wrapped paragraphs, with two
trailing spaces preserving a hard row break. Blank lines add paragraph
spacing; Markdown headings and lists end pending body paragraphs before
their own styled rows.

One-to-nine-digit `1.`/`1)` numbered items now retain
their literal label as exact authored glyph ink, indent continuations and
do not receive synthetic bullet strokes. Missing digit or separator shapes
refuse at native export. Long headings refuse their one-line fixed region.
This does **not** implement inline Markdown styling, nested lists,
semantic list identities or custom marker letterforms.

Only caller-authored exact glyph strokes render; missing visible shapes still
refuse with codepoints instead of silently substituting font characters.

Native regression tests rendered real two-page PDF and separate 72-DPI PNG
pages from a heading, paragraph, list and subsequent body lines. Additional
tests cover decomposed Unicode glyph wrapping without dividing a grapheme,
selected paper margins, exact file-vs-inline output, invalid UTF-8/oversize
file refusal, and immutable existing output on oversized headings, missing
shapes and insufficient explicitly requested pages.

Four further native regressions distinguish Markdown soft-wrapped Unicode
paragraphs from exact plain-text and explicit two-space hard breaks. They
verify preserved decomposed graphemes, heading/list boundaries, original
ink expansion, blank-line spacing, and one versus two physical pages for the
same 40 source lines.

Existing two-page PDF/PNG and seeded variation tests
now use deliberate hard breaks instead of relying on non-Markdown line
semantics. Real PDF/PNG parity and atomic empty-heading/list refusal are
also verified. This is still a restricted export-only parser.

Blank layout now distinguishes source formats: consecutive Markdown blank
lines collapse to a single 4-mm paragraph gap and leading/trailing blanks
add no space; plain-text blank rows reserve the entire 9-mm default pitch,
roll across actual page boundaries and count toward the 128-row budget.
Two native regressions verify page 2 physical ink, byte-identical PDF/PNG
for collapsed Markdown whitespace, distinct plain output, and immutable
existing PDF on excessive rows or an insufficient page selection.

Markdown heading placement also reserves one following visible row, including
an optional collapsed blank-line gap, when that combined span fits a page.
Near the foot of a sheet, the title and first paragraph/list row therefore
move together; an ending heading is not forced onto a new page.

On paper
with room for only one physical row, the limited compiler gracefully keeps
the heading and following content on successive pages rather than refusing.
Two native tests cover all three heading levels, exact page-local ornament
coordinates, actual two-page PDF/PNG and the one-row fallback. This is a
simple first-row orphan guard, not full multi-line widow/orphan control.

Three additional ordered-list tests verify mixed heading, decimal and
parenthesized numbering alongside bullet marks; native PDF and transparent
PNG use the same user-authored numerals and delimiters. Wrapped numbered
items retain their indentation without duplicating the label, and a
10-digit marker remains ordinary text instead of silently accepting an
unsupported list syntax. Missing number glyphs and empty numbered items
refuse before replacing an existing PDF. Numbering is not computed or
persisted in a semantic document.

A subsequent native hanging-indent correction measures each ordered item's
exact authored numeral, delimiter and separator spaces before wrapping.
Continuation rows start under the *item text*, not the numeric marker;
long first words retain at least one glyph next to the numeral. Two added
Rust CLI tests cover single/two-digit marker widths, extra authored spaces,
actual first-body/continuation ink coordinates, real PDF/PNG exports and a
valid narrow paper profile that refuses a label with no room for its first
item glyph. This does not introduce nested list semantics.

The authenticated browser's existing **Source material** textarea now has
an explicit `atrament-source.md` download for later `--flow-markdown-file`
use. JavaScript performs only a UTF-8 byte-bound transport guard (4,096
bytes), does not parse Markdown, and requires compatible session hydration.
The Rust localhost server serves one pinned new helper module, not a generic
file-serving route.

Firefox verifies the browser-created Blob exactly matches
the session's UTF-8 source with no new network request, and an existing
Firefox session-disposal regression passes with the added module. Four new
Node assertions cover byte-bound Unicode, NFD/BOM identity, malformed
surrogates and disabled initial controls. No browser PDF/PNG flow is implied.

The source and resulting ink are export-only CLI state, **not** a
persisted semantic notebook model.
Live browser Markdown rendering, stable semantic block identities,
complete Markdown,
fixed image regions, true glyph outlines, editable aesthetic title styles
and persisted notebook body content remain open.

This partial slice does not meet the finite two-page saved-notebook
acceptance criterion.

A further bounded Markdown blockquote slice supports one level of
`> text`/`>text` quote syntax. Adjacent quoted source lines join as a soft
paragraph with explicit two-space hard breaks, while changing to/from normal
text or structural blocks ends the quoted paragraph. The quote marker is
syntax, not a required handwriting glyph: Rust insets quote text by 6 mm
and adds one 0.4-mm vertical ink rule on each physical row, including
continuations and later physical pages. Empty quote blocks refuse atomically;
nested quote semantics remain unsupported.

Three Rust native tests assert
exact text/row positions and bar geometry, byte-identical PDF/PNG replay for
soft lines, different hard-break/ordinary ink, real two-page PDF and PNG,
plain-text `>` glyph refusal and preservation of a selected existing PDF.
This remains a CLI export-only subset, not persisted semantic quote blocks.

The subsequent native flow measurement correction derives statistical row
pitch from the parsed visible graphemes rather than the raw Markdown source:
syntax-only heading, unordered-list and quote markers do not contribute
their per-glyph height overrides, while plain-text literal markers and
authored numbered-list labels still do. Two Rust regressions validate exact
physical row positions and byte-identical PDF/PNG output using extreme
syntactic marker height overrides. No parser or saved-notebook state change
is claimed beyond this CLI pitch correction.

A bounded Markdown hard-break refinement recognizes one trailing
backslash on a body or quote source line as equivalent to two trailing
spaces, stripping the syntactic backslash before glyph measurement. Paired
backslashes and plain-text backslashes remain authored ink requirements;
empty syntactic blocks refuse atomically. Three Rust CLI regressions verify
source paragraph identity, two physical PDF pages and separate page-two
RGBA PNG, byte-identical native outputs against two-space breaks, strict
plain-text missing-glyph refusal, and row-pitch parity even with an extreme
custom backslash glyph-height override. This remains limited Markdown
grammar, not a general inline escaping implementation.

One further bounded aesthetic separator syntax recognizes at least three
contiguous `-`, `*` or `_` marks on an otherwise empty Markdown line.
Each variant emits the same single 0.4-mm native horizontal pen rule, up to
60 mm wide, and consumes exactly one measured row. Paragraph gaps, physical
page placement and the 128-row budget remain authoritative. Separator
syntax does not need authored marker glyphs, but the CLI flow requires at
least one visible text block; separator-only flow refuses explicitly.

Three Rust integration regressions verify exact micrometre geometry and
byte-identical PDF/PNG across spellings, blank-paragraph spacing, page-two
placement, and failure without replacing an existing selected PDF for
unsupported near-miss syntax or exceeded row/page budgets. No claim of
persistent semantic separator identity or a full Markdown compiler is made.

Follow-up native-output regressions also lock heading keep-with-next
when the next physical row is a thematic separator at a page boundary,
including the correct PDF content stream: heading and separator ink move
together rather than being split across pages. A mixed heading, quote,
backslash hard-break, separator and paragraph source also produces
byte-identical PDF and PNG for LF versus CRLF source line endings.
Both are narrow CLI composition guarantees, not persisted page blocks.

One optional title-decoration refinement adds
`--heading-style double-underline` with the same level-dependent pen
width (0.55 / 0.42 / 0.30 mm) as ordinary heading underlines. A second
colored pen stroke is drawn 0.9 mm above the original underline, both
beneath the author-supplied handwritten heading glyphs. This is bounded
to the Rust CLI, preserves single-underline bytes by default and retains
existing marker/none behavior.

Two native Rust integration tests check
each horizontal micrometre coordinate, hierarchy and exact custom ink
color, quote/separator ornament independence, PDF RGB paint count,
deterministic PNG replay and true page-two keep-with-next behavior;
duplicate style refusal is also covered. It does not establish a
separate saved semantic title style.

A bounded source-admission compatibility refinement treats one initial
UTF-8 U+FEFF BOM as transport metadata, not handwritten ink, for both
explicit inline text and caller-selected UTF-8 files. The raw 4,096-byte
limit includes the BOM; an interior or duplicate U+FEFF stays literal and
needs its own authored shape. Two Rust regressions confirm real PDF/PNG
byte parity for plain/Markdown, LF/CRLF, and real file import; invalid
BOM-only/over-limit source and interior unauthored glyphs refuse without
replacing an existing output. No other Unicode normalization is inferred.

A later opt-in fixed-area CLI slice accepts
`--flow-region LEFT_UM TOP_UM WIDTH_UM HEIGHT_UM` as positive-width and
positive-height absolute micrometre geometry, repeated on every physical
page. It checks overflow and the selected saved paper's actual printable
rectangle; the same native word/Unicode layout, heading ornaments,
synthetic bullets, pagination, authored glyph refusals and PDF/PNG ink
output then operate inside that bounded area.

Two native Rust tests
verify measured heading/list/body coordinates on both pages, native
PDF streams and separate transparent PNG pages, deterministic replay,
invalid or too-small area diagnostics and unchanged output on page-budget
or geometry refusal. Without this flag the original full-margin layout
remains byte-identical. This meets a limited fixed-text-region subcase,
**not** image-aware page obstacles, independently positioned regions,
persisted semantic block identities or end-to-end browser rendering.

## Fixed-region post-transform ink clearance (partial) — 2026-11-03

The CLI now revalidates **actual expanded** glyph and Markdown ornament
strokes against the explicitly selected `--flow-region` after all opt-in
mean-profile slant, per-occurrence variation and pen-thickness changes.
A line that fit nominal text advances but crosses the physical region
boundary (including half the actual pen width) refuses before writing
PDF or transparent PNG. All flow-generated page strokes are checked even
when the caller selects a PNG from another page.

Explicit `--ink-line`
overlays remain independent annotations and keep the native paper-wide
printable-region checks. Without a selected fixed region, prior output
behavior remains unchanged.

A failing-before-fix native test reproduced 50-degree slanted lettering
escaping a 25-mm-wide admitted region while staying inside the A4 paper.
Three direct accepted/refused paths check real PDF, page-one PNG refusal
for page-two overflow, immutable selected output files, and independent
manual ink outside the flow area. Existing two-page region PDF/PNG and
full CLI suites pass. This is bounded geometry admission, not semantic
block persistence, image-aware flow or arbitrary page regions.

## Indented and closed ATX headings (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native Markdown flow now admits zero to three leading ASCII spaces on
`#` through `######` headings, and ignores optional closing `#` sequences
preceded by a separator space. These are source syntax rather than glyphs.
A hash attached directly to the title or followed by non-hash text remains
literal and requires its own authored shape; four-space indents stay literal.
Empty headings still refuse before replacing a selected output.

Two independent native CLI integration tests compare byte-identical real PDF
and transparent PNG exports for ordinary and decorated heading syntax,
including exact NFC `ó` and NFD `o` plus combining acute. They also check
missing literal hashes, four-space indentation and empty headings refuse
without overwriting an existing PDF. Heading levels, glyph inks and marker
ornaments remain identical to the existing limited compiler. This is not a
full Markdown parser or a persisted semantic notebook identity.

## Literal Markdown line-prefix escapes (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native CLI's restricted Markdown subset now accepts a single backslash
before a line-leading `#`, `>`, `-`, `*`, `+` or `_` (after zero to three
indentation spaces). The backslash is syntax; the escaped mark remains
visible user-authored handwriting, not a heading, quote, bullet or rule.
Unsupported escapes and four-space-indented lines remain literal.

Native PDF and transparent PNG regressions compare indented and unindented
escaped marks using six distinct authored punctuation shapes plus accented
text. An unapproved literal mark or other backslash still refuses before
replacing an existing output. This is a deliberately limited line-prefix
escape, not complete CommonMark inline escaping or a saved notebook model.

## Indented Markdown thematic rules (partial) — 2026-11-04

The bounded Markdown CLI now treats `---`, `***`, and `___` thematic rules
with zero to three leading ASCII spaces as the same single physical rule.
Indentation does not require punctuation handwriting or change the emitted
PDF/PNG ink. Four-space indents stay literal, and separator-only documents
remain invalid without at least one visible authored text block.

Two native CLI regressions compare byte-identical PDFs and transparent PNGs
for all three spellings and one-to-three-space indentation, including the
three actual vector ink rules. Four-space indents and separator-only sources
refuse without changing a selected PDF. This does not add fenced code blocks,
full thematic-rule grammar or persistent decorative block identities.

## Indented Markdown bullets, quotes and numbered items (partial)

The bounded native CLI accepts zero to three leading ASCII spaces before
single-level `>` quotes, `-`/`*`/`+` bullets and one-to-nine-digit ordered
markers such as `1.` or `2)`. Syntax indentation adds no authored ink or
margin shift. Numbered labels remain exact handwriting glyphs, not inferred
numbering; quotes and unordered bullets retain their physical pen marks.
Four-space indents remain literal source and are not parsed as code blocks.

Two native PDF/transparent-PNG regressions check byte-for-byte equality
between ordinary and independently indented mixed quoted, bulleted and
ordered blocks using NFC and NFD accented ink plus four explicit numeral
and delimiter glyph strokes. They also prove four-space list/quote source
without authored punctuation refuses before overwriting an existing PDF.
This is still a flat, restricted Markdown compiler, not nested list parsing.

## Spaced Markdown thematic marks (partial) — 2026-11-04

The CLI's limited Markdown syntax now accepts up to three initial ASCII
spaces and interior ASCII spaces in thematic rules such as `- - -`,
`*  * *` and `_ _   _`. Three or more identical marks produce the same
single 0.4-mm native horizontal rule as unspaced `---`, `***` and `___`.
These marks do not need authored glyphs; they do not move adjacent writing.

Two native PDF/transparent-PNG regressions check identical physical bytes
with unspaced rules, exact rule counts, rejection of fewer/mixed marks,
escaped markers and four-space indents, and immutable selected PDF output
on refusal. A separator-only source still refuses without visible text.
This does not introduce nested blocks, arbitrary thematic syntax or
persisted notebook block identity.

## Literal fenced Markdown code rows (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native CLI now recognizes matching three-or-more backticks or tildes
(with up to three leading ASCII spaces) as a limited Markdown code fence.
An opening fence may carry one optional ASCII language token of up to 32
characters; it is metadata, not a handwriting glyph or style. A closing
fence must use the same marker with at least the opening count and may
contain only trailing ASCII spaces. Unclosed fences refuse before export.

Each content line inside a fence is literal authored handwriting: `#`,
`>`, list markers and other Markdown punctuation are no longer interpreted
as structure. Every source newline occupies a physical row, including
empty rows; long literal lines still obey native wrapping/page/ink limits.
Fence characters are syntax and need no authored glyphs, whereas visible
code punctuation requires an exact caller-authored glyph shape. No inferred
monospace font, syntax highlighting or hidden fallback is introduced.

Four native PDF/transparent-PNG integration tests cover byte-identical
plain-vs-fenced Unicode ink, backtick/tilde parity, literal marker colors,
restored heading/list syntax after a fence, page-two PNG, and strict
missing-glyph/unterminated-fence refusal and immutable selected output
on failure. This is a CLI-only literal code
block slice, not complete CommonMark or persisted semantic code identity.

## Ordinary Markdown paragraph indentation (partial) — 2026-11-04

The bounded Rust parser now treats zero to three leading ASCII spaces on an
ordinary Markdown paragraph line as syntactic, matching its existing
heading/list/quote indentation behavior. Those spaces no longer shift
handwriting or reduce the physical wrap width. At four spaces, source
spacing remains literal authored ink advance rather than an inferred code
block. Inside explicitly fenced code, all original leading spaces remain.

Two real PDF/transparent-PNG regressions compare accented multiline body
and heading ink against unindented Markdown, then verify that four-space
body text and two-space fenced code preserve exactly the same physical
position as plain text. Neither changes authored glyph shapes, fixed-region
refusals or persisted notebook structure.

## Heading keep-with-next skips non-printing fences — 2026-11-04

The native orphan guard now scans past code fence delimiters instead of
mistaking the opening marker for a following writing row. A terminal
heading followed by an empty fenced block therefore remains on the
last available physical row without manufacturing a second sheet.
When actual code text or a later paragraph follows, the guard still keeps
that first physical row with the heading across a page boundary.

Two native PDF/transparent-PNG regressions cover an exact last-row title
with an empty fence, equivalent visible code/text after syntax delimiters,
and page-two physical ink with preserved heading decoration.

## Escaped code fence punctuation (partial) — 2026-11-04

The bounded Markdown first-marker escape now admits a backslash before
leading backticks or tildes, in addition to the existing `#`, `>`, `-`,
`*`, `+` and `_`. A literal fence-like line consequently prints the
caller's exact punctuation glyphs instead of consuming the sequence as a
code block opener. Up to three leading syntax spaces remain inert, and
normal two-space hard line breaks still apply between such source rows.

Two native PDF/PNG tests compare literal plain-text punctuation to escaped
Markdown and require exact caller-authored shapes, with codepoint-specific
missing-glyph diagnostics and immutable preexisting output on refusal.
This does not add general inline Markdown escaping or create glyphs.

## Fixed-region fenced code native acceptance — 2026-11-04

Fenced literal punctuation, Unicode and an internal blank physical row
now have a native multi-page export regression under an explicitly selected
30,000-µm-high repeated flow region. The test compares PDF bytes and
transparent PNG pages 1–3 against the same authored lines in plain text,
then proves that an out-of-range page preserves the selected PNG and an
invalid too-short region preserves existing PDF bytes.
Code delimiters never consume region rows or bypass native constraints;
this remains one repeated fixed rectangle, not semantic per-block placement.

## Native checked and unchecked Markdown tasks (partial) — 2026-11-04

The bounded Rust Markdown flow now recognizes one flat task prefix:
`- [ ]`, `* [x]`, or `+ [X]`, plus the other bullet/checkbox combinations,
followed by a separator space and visible authored item text. Zero to three
leading spaces remain Markdown syntax. A 2.5-mm square of physical pen
strokes replaces the list bullet; checked tasks add a two-stroke check.

Only the first physical row gets the box, even when the body wraps onto
another PDF sheet. Caller-authored body glyphs remain exact and unchanged;
`[`, `]`, `x` and `X` do not require invented handwriting shapes.

Three native PDF/transparent PNG subprocess regressions count the actual
box/check ink and authored Unicode text, compare independent indented
spellings, and exercise the repeated fixed-region page-two export. A fenced
or escaped task prefix remains literal handwriting when its punctuation
is explicitly authored; malformed, four-space and empty task sources
refuse before replacing existing PDF output. Tasks are visual CLI-only
marks, not interactive persisted check states or nested Markdown lists.

A follow-up native regression proves that repeated separator spaces after
`[ ]` and `[x]` are syntax rather than 4-mm handwriting advances: the
same visible task body exports byte-identical PDF and PNG with one or
several spaces. A bare task marker now refuses as an empty item even when
literal bracket and checkmark glyph shapes have been authored, avoiding
accidental conversion into an ordinary bulleted handwriting line.

The public native CLI `notebook --help` now names checkbox syntax, and a
saved UTF-8 `.md` file with an initial BOM and CRLF line endings produces
the same checked/unchecked NFC/NFD task ink as inline Markdown in both
PDF and transparent PNG. No file contents are written or modified by
this read-only source admission path.

## Pasted horizontal tabs as bounded physical spacing — 2026-11-04

The native Rust flow-text and flow-markdown source admission now accepts
pasted horizontal tabs and expands each to the next four-column stop
measured in Unicode extended graphemes. This occurs after optional UTF-8
BOM removal and CRLF-to-LF handling, before any Markdown parsing or physical
layout. A decomposed vowel plus accent counts as one source column without
NFC/NFD normalization. Tabs do not require an invented glyph shape.

Original source and expanded source each independently obey the 4096-byte
cap; an expanded overflow or any other control character refuses before
a preexisting PDF can be replaced. Three real native subprocess regressions
compare PDF and transparent PNG bytes against authored ASCII-space source
for plain text, checked tasks, fenced literal text, and a saved BOM/CRLF
Markdown file with decomposed accents; they verify that source bytes remain
unchanged. Tab stops are logical grapheme columns, not measurements of
variable handwriting width or full CommonMark indentation semantics.

## Pasted Unicode line and paragraph separators — 2026-11-04

After CRLF handling, native source admission maps U+2028 line
separators to a hard Markdown line break (two spaces plus LF), and
U+2029 paragraph separators to two LFs before Markdown parsing.
The latter reserves a plain-text blank physical row or
one collapsed 4-mm Markdown paragraph gap. A source of only separators
still refuses as non-visible text; unrelated controls including U+0085
remain rejected. Neither separator needs invented handwriting glyph ink.

Real native PDF/transparent PNG regressions compare source separators
against matching hard breaks and blank-separated paragraphs in plain text,
Markdown heading/task content and a saved BOM/CRLF source. They check
fixed-region second-page output and unchanged input bytes, plus distinguish
a paragraph gap from one line break. This is source-boundary admission,
not a complete Unicode typography or paragraph identity engine.

## Tab-expansion complexity bound — 2026-11-04

Tab-free sources now skip additional grapheme-boundary traversal entirely.
Tabbed inputs traverse successive Unicode grapheme suffixes rather than
repeatedly requesting offsets from the start of the source. The new Rust
unit regression exercises exactly 4096 expanded UTF-8 bytes from 1024
tabbed cells, plus refusal for one additional cell.

Existing real native PDF/PNG cases still cover preserved NFC/NFD pen
identities and exported
spacing. This bounds source expansion to a forward-only scan without
changing the canonical line or physical output semantics.

## Literal escaped ordered labels (partial) — 2026-11-04

The bounded Markdown CLI recognizes an ordered prefix such as `1\. text`
or `12\) text` after zero to three ASCII indentation spaces. The single
backslash removes only the list interpretation: digits and delimiter remain
exact caller-authored handwriting, and the physical text uses the ordinary
paragraph inset rather than a numbered-list hanging indent. Unescaped
`1. text` and `2) text` retain their existing ordered layout. This is a
restricted line-prefix escape, not general inline Markdown formatting.

Two real Rust PDF/transparent-PNG subprocess regressions check byte-identical
ordinary plain-text and escaped Markdown for both delimiter kinds, physical
list/non-list distinction, and indentation neutrality. A missing numeral or
delimiter shape, unsupported punctuation escape and four-space indent refuse
without overwriting an existing selected PDF. The compiler still requires
caller-authored numeral and punctuation strokes and does not persist a
semantic paragraph/list identity.

## Literal escaped ordered labels (partial) — 2026-11-04

The bounded Markdown CLI recognizes an ordered prefix such as `1\. text`
or `12\) text` after zero to three ASCII indentation spaces. The single
backslash removes only the list interpretation: digits and delimiter remain
exact caller-authored handwriting, and the physical text uses the ordinary
paragraph inset rather than a numbered-list hanging indent. Unescaped
`1. text` and `2) text` retain their existing ordered layout. This is a
restricted line-prefix escape, not general inline Markdown formatting.

Two real Rust PDF/transparent-PNG subprocess regressions check byte-identical
ordinary plain-text and escaped Markdown for both delimiter kinds, physical
list/non-list distinction, and indentation neutrality. A missing numeral or
delimiter shape, unsupported punctuation escape and four-space indent refuse
without overwriting an existing selected PDF. The compiler still requires
caller-authored numeral and punctuation strokes and does not persist a
semantic paragraph/list identity. A separate limit regression compares actual
nine-digit escaped labels against plain handwritten PDF/PNG bytes; a
10-digit escape stays literal and refuses when its backslash was not
explicitly authored, without replacing the selected PDF.

## Six-level aesthetic Markdown headings (partial) — 2026-11-04

The bounded native CLI now parses ATX heading levels `####`, `#####` and
`######` as well as the existing `#`, `##` and `###`. It retains the
previous zero-to-three-space indentation, optional space-delimited closing
hashes, one-line fixed-region refusal and heading/first-row page keep.
None of the syntax hashes require authored glyphs; seven hashes remain
literal and require their own strokes.

Deeper levels use progressively lighter native highlights: level 4 gets
two 0.76-mm marker strokes, level 5 one 0.62-mm stroke, and level 6 one
0.50-mm stroke. Single/double-underline width falls to 0.24, 0.18 and
0.12 mm, respectively. Title text still uses caller-authored shapes, and
marker scale and separate heading-ink controls retain their normal limits.

Two native integration tests compare real PDF and transparent PNG from
ordinary versus indented/closed headings, assert exact marker counts and
unchanged ink, exercise underline/double-underline/none output, and refuse
unsupported seven-hash and unapproved empty/indented forms without changing
an existing PDF. A further native regression exercises H4/H5/H6 orphan
prevention across page two, checking marker strokes in the second PDF stream
and the same selected-page PNG with and without fenced row syntax.
This extends CLI-only rendering: it does not create
persistent semantic title identities, inline styling or a general parser.

## Forward-only Unicode measurement (partial) — 2026-11-04

Native pasted-source tab expansion, per-word advance measurement and
height/baseline pitch estimation now share one bounded next-grapheme
reader. Each consumes the remaining UTF-8 suffix, rather than repeatedly
