Those literal equals marks still require authored ink,
and a 25-mm glyph-height override must reserve 29-mm physical row pitch.
An ordinary `a\n===` source still uses heading syntax and no equals glyph.
An unescaped `---` following a structural break becomes a thematic rule,
whereas `\---` remains exact authored dash ink with the same 29-mm pitch.

Real native PDF and transparent PNG exports independently verify two physical
pages in a one-row fixed region, exact first/second-page authored ink,
LF-equivalent source bytes, and unchanged accepted files after missing-glyph
or insufficient-page refusal. This adds an interoperation regression for
pasted Unicode source, not a persistent semantic Markdown document; the
finite saved-notebook acceptance criterion remains open.

A further deterministic boundary regression combines Markdown structural
markers in two positions with eight possible trailing contexts, including
Setext candidates, escaped equals, quotes, lists, fences and explicit source
breaks. Over 3,000 admitted cases check that an unusually tall exact `=`
glyph determines the native physical pitch if and only if it reaches real
